1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 d5 4. exd5 Qxd5 5. d4 Bg4 6. Be2 Nf6 $5 {(Having played ...?c6 so early, Black must either exchange with 6...cxd4 or take a little gamble. 6...e6 also gives White the option of 7.h3 ?h5 8. c4 followed by d5)} 7. O-O (7. h3 Bh5 8. c4 $5 Qd6 9. d5 Bxf3 10. Bxf3 Nd4 11. Nc3 e5 $5 12. O-O O-O-O {is critical, when it is a question of how exposed the Black ? may become on the ?-side)}) 7... e6 8. Be3 Be7 $6 {(After ?e3 in these lines, Black should almost always exchange central pawns with ...cxd4, as O'Shaughnessy now succinctly illustrates)} 9. h3 Bh5 10. dxc5 $1 Qxd1 11. Rxd1 Nd5 12. b4 O-O 13. a3 a5 14. b5 Na7 (14... Nxe3 15. fxe3 Na7 {appears to successfully undermine the c5 pawn, but White can advance with} 16. c6 bxc6 17. b6 $1 Nb5 18. g4 Bg6 19. Ne5 {with advantage}) 15. g4 Bg6 16. Bd4 f6 17. c4 Nf4 18. Bf1 Rad8 19. Nc3 Nc8 20. Be3 e5 21. Nd5 Ne6 {(Still hoping to regain the c5 pawn, inadvisedly gambited on move 7)} 22. Nxe7+ Nxe7 23. Rd6 Kf7 24. Rad1 Rc8 25. Rd7 Nxc5 26. Bxc5 Rxc5 {(As ?xb7? fails to the skewer ?e4. So finally Black has regained his pawn, but at the cost of allowing both White rooks to penetrate)} 27. R1d6 $1 Rb8 28. Nd2 b6 29. Bg2 Ke8 30. Rb7 Rxb7 31. Bxb7 Nc8 32. Bc6+ $5 (32. Bxc8 Rxc8 33. Rxb6 Rd8 {gives counterplay}) 32... Ke7 33. Rd7+ Ke6 34. Rb7 Ne7 35. Bd7+ $2 (35. Bh1 $1 Nc8 36. Bd5+ Kd6 37. Rxg7 {keeps control, as on} Ne7 {there comes} 38. Rxg6 Nxg6 39. Ne4+ Kc7 40. Nxc5) 35... Kd6 36. Bf5 Bxf5 37. gxf5 Rc7 38. Rxb6+ Kc5 39. Ra6 Nxf5 $2 (39... Rd7 $1 {Black's last chance to make a draw - Conquest} 40. Ne4+ Kxc4 41. Rxa5 Nxf5) 40. Kf1 $1 {(I had overlooked this move, coming across to defend the ? on d2 - Conquest)} Kd4 (40... Nd6 {draws, but with such an active ? Conquest suddenly has ambitions of playing for the win)} 41. a4 {(I don't see why ?d6 draws - what is Black's next after 41.a4? - Conquest)} Nb7 {(Holds the position - BCM)}) 41. Rxa5 Kc3 42. Ke1 Rd7 43. b6 Nd4 $2 (43... Rxd2 $1 {is in fact possible;} 44. b7 Rc2 (44... Rb2 $2 45. Rb5 $18) 45. Rd5 $1 (45. b8=Q $4 Rc1+ 46. Ke2 Nd4+ 47. Ke3 Re1#) (45. Kf1 {(? Conquest; Black's defensive idea is elusive to say the least)} Rc1+ 46. Kg2 Nh4+ 47. Kg3 $1 (47. Kh2 Nf3+ $1 48. Kg3 Nd4 $3) 47... g5 $1 48. Kg4 $1 $18) 45... Nd4 $1 46. Rxd4 $1 exd4 $1 47. b8=Q Rc1+ 48. Ke2 d3+ 49. Kf3 d2 50. Qb4+ Kc2 51. Qa4+ Kc3 52. Qb4+ {with a draw by perpetual check}) 44. Rd5 Re7 45. c5 f5 46. c6 {Source: BCM, June 1996, p306} 1-0

